is centred, so the model may contain an intercept. The nodewise lasso that
estimates the matrix Z uses one tuning parameter for all regressions,
chosen by 10-fold cross-validation (or with the Z&Z rule if \code{do.ZnZ = TRUE}). For \code{family = "binomial"}, the method is applied to the linearised
(IRLS) working model of a logistic lasso fit, as in hdi.
}
\section{Compatibility with hdi 0.1-10}{

For the same data, arguments and random seed (and \code{robust.divisor = "n"}
if \code{robust = TRUE}), \code{lasso.proj()} returns the same \code{pval}, \code{pval.corr},
\code{bhat}, \code{se}, \code{betahat} and \code{sigmahat} as \code{hdi::lasso.proj()} and leaves
the random number generator in the same state; this is verified against
the archived hdi package (with the same version of glmnet;
\code{multiplecorr.method = "WY"} also depends on the BLAS/LAPACK used by
\code{MASS::mvrnorm()}). The differences are:
\itemize{
\item \code{robust = TRUE}: the robust standard error uses the divisor
\eqn{n - \hat s}{n - s} of equation (5) of Dezeure, Bühlmann and Zhang
(2017) by default (\code{robust.divisor = "n-s"}), so its standard errors are
larger by the factor \eqn{\sqrt{n/(n-\hat s)}}{sqrt(n / (n - s))} and
its p-values are at least as large as hdi's. hdi divided by n (Section
3.3.2 of the paper); \code{robust.divisor = "n"} reproduces hdi exactly.
Results with \code{robust = FALSE} (the default) are not affected.
\item The result has class \code{c("silm_lasso_proj", "silm_proj")} instead of
\code{"hdi"}, and the elements \code{groupTest} and \code{clusterGroupTest} (functions
for group tests in hdi) are \code{NULL}: those tests are not provided. The
draws hdi made to prepare them are still consumed from the random number
stream unless \code{suppress.grouptesting = TRUE}, so later results in a
session are unaffected.
\item \code{family = "binomial"}: hdi removed the intercept of the working model by
mean-centring, which leaves it in the model when the weights vary and
biases the estimates and the confidence intervals. SILM projects out the
intercept direction \eqn{\sqrt{w}} instead. Use \code{legacy = TRUE} to obtain
hdi's results. For \code{family = "binomial"} a logical or two-level factor
response is also accepted (with or without \code{legacy}); for \code{"gaussian"}, a
logical response is used as 0/1, as in hdi.
\item Arguments are checked before the computations start.
}

Documented properties of hdi that are kept: a numeric \code{betainit} refers to
the centred and (if \code{standardize = TRUE}) scaled design; with the "cv lasso"
initial fit, \code{sigmahat} is \eqn{\|y - \hat y\|/\sqrt{n - \hat s - 1}}{||y - yhat|| /
sqrt(n - s - 1)}
(the intercept of the glmnet fit is counted); the WY adjustment uses the
homoscedastic covariance \code{crossprod(Z)} even when \code{robust = TRUE}, and its
adjusted p-values can be 0 (below the Monte Carlo resolution 1/N).
}

